<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chremonidean War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 267–261 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chremonidean War (c. 267–261 BCE) was a conflict in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and their allies, backed by Ptolemy II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, tried to end Macedonian domination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Named after the Athenian politician Chremonides, who proposed the alliance, it ended with the defeat of the Spartan king Areus I near Corinth and the surrender of Athens to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-ii-gonatas">Antigonus II Gonatas</a> after a long siege.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chremonidean-war.jpg" alt="Chremonidean War" width="320" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: T8612 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chremonidean_War_-_First_Stage_(267-265_BC).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
